00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:37.280
The web sort of outgrew the idea that every user&nbsp;
should fetch every asset directly from the origin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:44.240
And so the CDN was born. The CDN became the&nbsp;
internet's performance and reliability fabric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:51.280
providing intelligent routing, distributed edges,&nbsp;
observability, and so so much more. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:56.560
AI use cases have now sort of outgrown the idea&nbsp;
that every token should be fetched directly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.560 --> 00:21:01.920
the model provider. Tokens have actually become&nbsp;
a production dependency now. We don't just use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:08.160
them during dev. We use them all the time. And&nbsp;
the model labs are becoming the new origins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:15.280
They're powerful, but they're also expensive, rate&nbsp;
limited, and both geographically and operationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:24.320
variable. As our friends at the model labs&nbsp;
know, this is a really hard problem at scale.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:30.720
And so that's why we built the AI gateway. It&nbsp;
routes around failures. It serves tokens reliably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:37.280
through the same global network that Vercel has&nbsp;
run for over a decade. It also simplifies auth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:43.120
centralizes usage and spend tracking with&nbsp;
granular observability and offers zero data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:49.360
retention. But there's another important problem&nbsp;
that the AI gateway solves, which is model choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:54.720
Today's agents don't actually use a single model&nbsp;
architecture anymore. They use many models from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.720 --> 00:22:00.080
many providers. Our AI gateway production index&nbsp;
recently showed that teams running agents at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:05.360
scale route across 35 different models. So model&nbsp;
choice isn't just about experimentation anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:12.400
It's how you actually run AI in production at&nbsp;
scale. Serhant is one of the most recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.520
names in real estate. And they use Vercel's AI&nbsp;
gateway to build an AI app that helps their team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:23.600
with property valuation, marketing, and client&nbsp;
communication. They use different models for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:30.400
different tasks based on uh the need for power or&nbsp;
if they're trying to watch cost. Claude performs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:37.280
complex market analysis. GPT is used to create&nbsp;
marketing copy and Gemini generates images. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:45.040
all of this happens with a single AI gateway API&nbsp;
key. AI gateway is already serving over 1 trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:51.280
tokens per day across hundreds of models from&nbsp;
dozens of providers. It's a huge part of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:59.840
makes Vercel the open platform for AI. Okay, so&nbsp;
Vercel now serves pixels and tokens instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:06.800
and reliably, but modern software doesn't really&nbsp;
follow a simple request response model anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:13.760
Agents may need to run for minutes, hours, or&nbsp;
even days and across many, many complex tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:19.120
So that's where the next tool in the agent&nbsp;
stack comes in, which is the workflow SDK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:23.920
Longrunning workflows and back-end&nbsp;
jobs are nothing new in software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:30.000
but they're one of the most challenging things&nbsp;
to build. Things can go wrong, and they do. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.160
I'm sure many of you have experienced timeouts,&nbsp;
dropped state, dropped connections, and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:41.520
Without a primitive for durability, you have to&nbsp;
kind of handstitch together things like retries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:47.760
state persistence, and this can get really&nbsp;
ugly. This is why we built the workflow SDK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:52.880
We like to say it provides infinite compute&nbsp;
durability. It enables you to build longrunning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.880 --> 00:24:00.160
apps and agents that automatically suspend,&nbsp;
resume, retry, and maintain state with ease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:06.480
DoorDash uses the workflow SDK to run traditional&nbsp;
ETL jobs, keeping data in their app up to date.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:12.800
And our customer Flora built an entire AI design&nbsp;
platform on the workflow SDK to help designers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:19.440
generate visual content at scale. Inside their&nbsp;
platform, agents fan out across 50 different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:26.320
image models to generate visual directions from&nbsp;
a single creative brief. Workflow SDK checkpoints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:32.400
every step of every agent and pauses when the job&nbsp;
needs human input. And because every failure is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:39.760
automatically retried, the designer never needs to&nbsp;
start over. The whole idea behind agents is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:44.960
they can solve complex tasks across multi-step&nbsp;
workflows. And what we found is that one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:51.600
primary ways agents want to accomplish tasks&nbsp;
these days is by writing and executing code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:57.760
That's where our next agent stack primitive comes&nbsp;
in Vercel sandbox. LLMs have gotten really good at&nbsp;&nbsp;

